ely that is a right you know as  it exists on paper but it's not enforced  by the government you can be fired for  anything and that's one thing that was  really brought home to me and these  experiences how easy it is to be fired  for breaking a little rule like a  Walmart you can't say damn or hell and  you can imagine the other words you  can't say either and you know and I have  read I did not experience it's the  people who were involved in union  organizing have been fired Walmart  because if that's but that's illegal so  what they're fired for is saying damn  you know and so those all those little  rules the afl-cio estimates that 10,000  American workers are fired every year  for union activity that is that means  we're not that that right doesn't really  exist so those would be some of my  policy changes let's see here's somebody  he says Corbin somewhere it is here  he says wonderful thank you then he says  next time do it without a car Corbin you  do it without a car all right I mean I  would see these people you know standing  at the bus stops at the edge to see the  jobs are often in the periphery of the  Twin Cities some of the cheap housing is  in the center I would see these people  who would have you know an hour commute  each day I that I I can't imagine how I  would have done that  see you mentioned some of the advantages  you had in seeking and maintaining  employment did you notice any  differences faced by workers of color  and I can't say I was in the position to  survey that I would say what I think I  did notice was that when I was initially  applying at these big discount hotels  for hotel housekeeping jobs some reason  I thought that was going to be easier  than waitressing I would always be  steered toward waitressing and I think  that was because I was white and  english-speaking but I you know and  actually if if it had been a better  season when I was working it would have  been more lucrative to be in waitressing  but that was the ext